Given two strings str1 and str2, find the length of their longest common substring.
A substring is a contiguous sequence of characters within a string.

Example 1:
Input: str1 = "abcde", str2 = "abfce"
Output: 2
Explanation: The longest common substring is "ab", which has a length of 2.

Example 2:
Input: str1 = "abcdxyz", str2 = "xyzabcd"
Output: 4
Explanation: The longest common substring is "abcd", which has a length of 4.

n=str1.length
m=str2.length
1<= n, m <=103
str1 and str2 are in lowercase alphabet

Approach:
The solution to this problem is unlike longest common subsequence. Here we run two for loops which represents the start
of each substring of str1 and str2. So for every substring of str1 that starts at i all the substrings of str2 starting
at every j position are checked if any of these substrings match. Until there is a match of characters continuously the
end pointers keep incrementing in the recursion. The recursion also stops if it goes beyond any of the lengths of the
string. When there is a match the curr length is increased by 1 and the remaining problem is given to the recursion. When
there is no match then the curr length is given 0. and after every comparison the curr length is compared to the max until
now. Writing the iterative brute force helps in further writing the recursive naive approach.

Naive approach -> TC = O(n * m * min(n,m)), SC = O(min(n,m)) (recursion stack space)
Memoization -> TC = O(n * m), SC = O(n * m) + O(min(n,m) (recursion stack space) = O(n * m)
Tabulation -> TC = O(n * m), SC = O(n * m)
Tabulation_SpaceOptimisation_1 -> TC = O(n * m), SC = O(2 * m) = O(m)
Tabulation_SpaceOptimisation_2 -> TC = O(n * m), SC = O(m)